Patch 06c: orientation partition pruning, resolution-scaled. Supersedes
patches 06 and 06b -- apply this INSTEAD of either, never on top.

Round 1 measured patch 06 across all four presets:

    Speed 4   A1 +14.00% / +0.75% (18.7)   A2 +18.52% / +1.29% (14.4)
    Speed 3   A1 +12.85% / +0.86% (14.9)   A2 +18.21% / +1.48% (12.3)
    Speed 2   A1 +14.77% / +0.81% (18.2)   A2 +18.81% / +1.50% (12.5)
    Speed 1   A1 +14.85% / +0.96% (15.5)   A2 +18.97% / +1.48% (12.8)

Speed and BD-rate are both essentially flat across presets while the bar rises
20 -> 35, so Speed 4 is the only preset worth targeting. There it needs A2's
BD-rate cut from 1.29% to 0.926% at unchanged speed, a 28% reduction; A1 needs
only 6.7%.

Three changes, in descending order of how well the data supports them.

1. Resolution-scaled minimum block size (new in 06c).

   All four presets agree that the pruning costs far more BD-rate on A2 (1080p,
   1.29%-1.50%) than on A1 (4K, 0.75%-0.96%) while delivering a HIGHER speedup
   there (18.2%-19.0% vs 12.8%-14.8%). Four independent measurements agreeing
   makes that a property of the heuristic: at 1080p it fires more often and is
   more often wrong.

   Block size in pixels explains it. A 16x16 block at 1080p covers about four
   times the scene area of a 16x16 block at 4K, so it holds more distinct
   content, more such blocks look directional under a block-scale profile, and
   more of the partitions pruned on that basis were genuinely useful. The
   original fixed 16-pixel floor therefore means something different at each
   resolution. It now scales: 16 at 4K and above, 32 at 1080p/720p, 64 below.

   Note what this targets. A1 (3840 wide) keeps a floor of 16 and is unchanged;
   A2 (1920 wide) moves to 32. The change acts on exactly the class that needs
   the 28% cut, and leaves alone the class that needs 6.7%.

2. Absolute variance floor (carried over from 06b).

   The orientation test is a ratio, so on a flat block it compares noise against
   noise: var_rows=8 against var_cols=1 triggered a prune on no evidence.
   Flat blocks settle on PARTITION_NONE quickly, so declining to prune them
   should return BD-rate at very little speed cost -- the best kind of change
   for a ratio, since it removes prunes that were cheap in speed and real in
   quality cost.

3. Frame-aware evidence bar, now narrowed (revised from 06b).

   In 06b this was justified by A2 scoring worse than A1 while pruning more,
   read as loss persisting through a longer GOP. That reading assumed A2
   differed from A1 only in frame count. It does not: A2 is 1080p, A1 is 4K, so
   resolution and GOP length are confounded and the comparison does not
   establish a propagation effect. Change 1 targets the part of the gap the
   data does support.

   What survives is the ordinary argument that frames many others predict from
   deserve more search. That justifies protecting the key frame, not the whole
   top of the pyramid, so the gate is narrowed: pruning is now disabled only at
   layer_depth 0, with layers 1 and 2 merely held to stricter bars (4x and 2x)
   instead of being disabled outright. This should recover speedup that 06b was
   giving away for a reason I could not defend.

Why not simply tighten the anisotropy threshold: tightening reduces pruning,
which lowers BD-rate AND speedup together, so the ratio only improves if
BD-rate falls faster than speed does. A blunt threshold move tends to shift
both in proportion and leave the ratio near 18.7 with less speed to show for
it. Each change above instead removes a class of prune that was expensive in
quality and cheap in speed, which is what actually moves a ratio.

All thresholds are overridable at build time for sweeping, e.g.
-DPART_PROFILE_MIN_VAR=128. They are first calibrations, not tuned results.

